        <common:reviewDetails>The study is very representative from a geographical and technological point of view. From a temporal point of view, as the data refer to the 2020, the representativeness is considered very good. For this transport typology, the completeness is considered good.&#13;
 &#13;
&#13;
ISPRA secondary data available in the database of average emission factors for road transport in Italy were used for modelling this mode of transport. The database of average emission factors for road transport is based on estimates made for the purpose of preparing the national inventory of atmospheric emissions reported in 2022, carried out annually by Ispra as a tool for verifying international commitments on the protection of the atmospheric environment, such as the Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC), the Kyoto Protocol, the Geneva Convention on Transboundary Air Pollution (UNECE-CLRTAP), and the European Directives on limiting emissions. Estimates are prepared by ISPRA on the basis of national input data on vehicle fleets and circulation (fleet numbers, average mileage and fuel consumption, speed by vehicle category with reference to urban, suburban and highway driving cycles, other specific national parameters). Emission factors are calculated both with respect to kilometers driven and with respect to consumption, with reference to both the detail of technologies and aggregation by sector and fuel, elaborated both at the total level and separately for the urban, extra-urban and highway spheres.&#13;
For the weight of cargo transported by trucks (tonnage), a literature search was conducted through which it was found that trucks transporting goods overland generally travel at a load capacity of 57%: "Freight trucks often travel empty or at less than full load. An estimated 20-35% of truck miles in the United States are driven empty. Those that are not empty have an average load factor of 57%."&#13;
&#13;
&#13;
The inventory data relating to the various processes within the system's boundaries are therefore to be considered reasonably representative of the supply chain under study, as they can to all intents and purposes be considered average data for the same. The quality of the data used in the study is considered to be good and the data were for the most part measured; where they were calculated or came from literature, they were all checked by experts.&#13;
Allocation was avoided as far as possible by dividing the unitary process into two or more sub-processes and collecting environmental data on these sub-processes.&#13;
When allocation could not be avoided, as in the case of products obtained during processing, the inputs and outputs of the system were divided between its different products and by-products.&#13;
The allocation was generally based on mass (e.g. for waste treatment, waste water and energy used).&#13;
The review  was performed  in compliance with Arcadia Metodology: “Metodologia per gli studi di filiera della Banca Dati italiana LCA” and ISO 14040-44 norms. The dataset has been assessed limited to the foreground data collected during the supply chain studies.&#13;
Each dataset has been evaluated based on the following 4 criteria:&#13;
1. Technological Representativeness;  2. Time Representativeness; 3. Geographical Representativeness; 4. Reliability (A)&#13;
In this contest the Reliability is very good.</common:reviewDetails>
